Hey [~accountid:5dde532fcdc2d20d05377446] 

Thank you for raising a ticket, I have now looked at the Driver T&Cs to see if Bolt is permitted to block drivers from using the platform if cancellation fees are abused. 

The following T&Cs can be relied on (highlighted in blue)



* *Section 7 Acceptable Use Policy*

a. This Section describes the Acceptable Use Policy and applies when any Service Provider is online on the Service Provider App.

b. {color:#4c9aff}You must not and you must ensure that all other Service Providers using the Bolt Platform in association with your account must not{color}:

iii. {color:#4c9aff}modify, interfere with{color}, disrupt or hack {color:#4c9aff}the Bolt Platform{color}, including introducing any software viruses or use the Bolt Platform to{color:#4c9aff} commit any fraud against Customers, the Bolt Parties or any third-parties{color} (like credit card issuer, holder, etc.);

viii. {color:#4c9aff}cause or allow a breach of any Policy{color};

c. N{color:#4c9aff}ot complying with the Acceptable Use Policy is a serious breach of these Terms and will entitle Bolt UK to take any of the following actions{color} (with or without notice, subject always to Section 9):

i.{color:#4c9aff} immediate, temporary or permanent withdrawal of your right to use the Bolt Platform{color};

ii. immediate, temporary or permanent removal of any User Content;

iii. {color:#4c9aff}issuing a notification to you{color};



* *Section 9 When our relationship starts, how use of the Bolt Platform may be disabled and how our relationship can come to an end*

Clause 9(c). {color:#4c9aff}Bolt UK is entitled to temporarily suspend or restrict use of, or access to, all or part of the Bolt Platform{color}:

iv. where the {color:#4c9aff}Bolt Platform is used by or in association with your account in such a way that the Acceptable Use Policy has not been followed{color}, {color:#4c9aff}the applicable Minimum Service Standards have not been met{color}, or where any unlawful or criminal act has been committed; or

ii. {color:#4c9aff}a serious or repeated breach of the Terms including the Acceptable Use Policy and applicable Minimum Platform Service Standards{color}; or

e. {color:#4c9aff}Bolt UK can terminate its relationship with you in its entirety, or in respect of a specific Service and remove your access or any access in association with your account to all or part of the Bolt Platform {color}(including preventing you from registering a new account) {color:#4c9aff}+by giving you at least 30 days' notice together with Bolt UK’s reasons for doing so+{color}. Such reasons may include (without limitation):

ii. {color:#4c9aff}you are in of breach of these Terms{color};



* *Section 20 Minimum Service Standards: Journeys*

d. {color:#4c9aff}Actions which are not consistent with the Minimum Service Standards can include {color}(but are not limited to):

i. failure by a Driver to proceed to the Passenger's pickup location in accordance with these Private Hire Terms once the Driver has elected to fulfil a Journey;

ii. actions giving rise to concerns of safety, health or conduct from Passengers;

iii. {color:#4c9aff}repeated and consistent cancellation of Journeys by a Driver prior to arriving at the pickup location but after they have elected to fulfil a Journey{color};

iv. {color:#4c9aff}cancellation of a Journey by a Driver after they have elected to fulfil a Journey without attempting to contact the Passenger{color}.



* +*Fraud policy*+ 

# Prohibited Behaviour

{color:#4c9aff}Behaviour that will be considered by Bolt to be Prohibited Behaviour is set out below{color}:

3.1. providing or allowing (including any attempt to provide or allow) any inaccurate, misleading, duplicate or false Information to the Bolt Parties, the Bolt Platform and/or any Users relating to the provision, carrying out, time, duration or location of any service requested or provided via the Bolt Platform;

3.2. using or allowing (including any attempt to use or allow) any device to be used for the falsification and/or manipulation of Information provided to the Bolt Parties, the Bolt Platform and/or any Users;

3.3. registering (including any attempt to register) as a User of the Bolt Platform using any inaccurate, misleading, duplicate or false Information;

3.4. allowing any person to use the Bolt Platform via personal login details that do not belong to them;

3.5. {color:#4c9aff}requesting or agreeing to provide services via the Bolt Platform in a manner that suggests there is not a genuine intention to have those services performed in full (for example services are repeatedly requested, cancelled by a User or otherwise not fulfilled as anticipated{color});



+*TO SUMMARISE*+ 

Bolt drivers are bound by the T&Cs and the Fraud Policy. Therefore, if a Driver is breaching the T&Cs and/or the Fraud policy, Bolt will be able to rely on the T&Cs to take action, whether that be *notifying* the Driver or *temporarily or permanently removing* the Driver from the platform. 

However, please note, if Bolt decides to proceed with blocking Drivers and removing all access from the platform, the process in the T&Cs will need to be followed by Bolt when doing so. Therefore, you must give a Driver 30 days noticed (as per section 9.(e)) with Bolts reason for doing so , this is the “statement of reasons”. Essentially the “statement of reasons” will contain a reference to the T&Cs which allows Bolt to exercise this right. 

If you are proceeding with blocking the Drivers who are doing this, I would make sure that you have tangible evidence supporting this decision. Ensuring that you have evidence on file will help Bolt mitigate against any civil claims that the Driver could ‘potentially’ make against Bolt (especially if they are blocked). 

Keeping hold of evidence will mean that Bolt can defend its position (if a claim was made or if the Driver complains to Bolt and disputes the block), and whilst this is more of a  behavioural issue the Fraud Policy could also be relied on (section 3.5 says “{color:#4c9aff}there is not a genuine intention to have those services performed in full{color}“) it could be argued that the Driver has no “genuine intention” to perform services.  

To summarise, I would suggest if you are taking any action against Drivers to ensure that this is outlined clearly giving the Driver the requisite notice outlined in the T&Cs and ensuring that a Statement of Reasons is provided, I would also keep any evidence on file that supports that this driver is in fact abusing cancellation fees. 

Happy to discuss further if needed, please reach out to me on slack. 

Thank you

